  [PASS] 117E (the item's deliverable, WITHIN channel) inside a galaxy the acceleration relation is followed to an intrinsic 0.036 +- 0.008 dex -- tighter than the RAR's own quoted scatter, and the tightest bound in this hunt.  It is NOT zero, at 4.5 sigma, but 0.036 dex in g is about 4% in v, which is the size of the disc-geometry correction this estimator deliberately omits: item 23's spherical relation g_obs = nu(g_bar/a_0) g_bar is not the AQUAL/QUMOND answer for a flattened disc.  So this is an UPPER BOUND on intrinsic scatter and cannot be pushed lower without a proper disc solve   (observed within 0.0569 +- 0.0050, budget 0.0441 -> intrinsic 0.0359 +- 0.0079 dex (4.6 sigma from zero, 95% upper bound 0.0478); alt footing 0.0365)
  [PASS] 117F (the item's deliverable, BETWEEN channel -- AGAINST INTEREST) the framework's prediction of ZERO intrinsic scatter FAILS in the normalisation channel.  On the best-distance galaxies a per-galaxy offset of 0.079 dex survives the whole observational budget, with a 95% lower bound of 0.04 dex.  a_0 universal plus the quoted errors plus a 0.11 dex stellar-population Upsilon scatter is NOT enough to put these galaxies on one curve   (observed between 0.1150 +- 0.0147, budget 0.0848 -> intrinsic 0.0777 dex (95% lower bound 0.0326); alt footing 0.0834)
  what the 0.079 dex costs, two ways:
     as stellar M/L: the Upsilon term would have to rise from 0.11 dex to 0.19 dex of galaxy-to-galaxy scatter (1.7x the stellar-population value)
     as a_0: with the sample's median kernel slope n = -0.39 at y = 0.24, a per-galaxy a_0 scatter of 0.20 dex would do it -- which the framework forbids
  [PASS] 117G the residual has exactly two readings and the framework is committed to one of them: either the galaxy-to-galaxy stellar M/L scatter is 1.7x the stellar-population value, or a_0 varies from galaxy to galaxy by 0.23 dex.  The framework forbids the second, so it OWES the first -- an independent Upsilon measurement at 10% would settle it, which is the same conclusion items 76 and 100 reached from the other side   (Upsilon scatter would need to be 0.19 dex (SPS: 0.11); a_0 scatter would need to be 0.20 dex (framework: 0))

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE LambdaCDM ALTERNATIVE, COMPUTED HERE: how much RAR scatter do halo mass and concentration make?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  each galaxy gets the NFW halo that reproduces its OUTERMOST measured point at the mean c(M200) of Dutton & Maccio (2014);
  M200 is then scattered by the stellar-to-halo-mass scatter and c by its cosmological 0.11 dex, and the induced RAR
  residual is decomposed into the same two channels.  This is the halo-to-halo scatter LambdaCDM cannot avoid.
  sigma(log M200) = 0.08, sigma(log c) = 0.11:  LambdaCDM RAR scatter  within 0.0157 dex   between 0.0711 dex
  sigma(log M200) = 0.16, sigma(log c) = 0.11:  LambdaCDM RAR scatter  within 0.0210 dex   between 0.0797 dex
  sigma(log M200) = 0.25, sigma(log c) = 0.11:  LambdaCDM RAR scatter  within 0.0280 dex   between 0.0969 dex
  [PASS] 117H AGAINST INTEREST -- the unexplained offset is exactly the size LambdaCDM predicts for free.  Scattering M200 by 0.16 dex and c by its cosmological 0.11 dex produces 0.083 dex of between-galaxy RAR scatter and almost none within galaxies -- the same shape and the same size as the residual the framework has to charge to the mass-to-light ratio.  On the between channel LambdaCDM's prediction lands on the observed value with no extra freedom, and the framework's does not   (observed between 0.1150 +- 0.0147; framework budget alone 0.0848; framework budget + LambdaCDM halo scatter 0.1164.  LambdaCDM within-galaxy prediction 0.0210 against an observed 0.0569, so the WITHIN channel does not discriminate either way)
  [PASS] 117I AGAINST BOTH MODELS AND AGAINST MY OWN ESTIMATOR -- the within-galaxy channel excludes the framework's zero (4.5 sigma) AND LambdaCDM's 0.021 dex (1.9 sigma), which means it is not discriminating between them but measuring something neither includes.  The obvious candidate is the modelling error common to both: the spherical algebraic relation applied to a flattened disc, plus non-circular motions and beam smearing.  So the between-galaxy channel is where the two theories differ, and there they are degenerate -- the framework charging 0.19 dex to Upsilon, LambdaCDM charging 0.083 dex to halo scatter.  This item delivers a BOUND, not a discrimination   (within-galaxy intrinsic 0.0359 +- 0.0079: 4.6 sigma above the framework's 0 and 1.9 sigma above LambdaCDM's 0.0210.  Between-galaxy intrinsic 0.0777 +- 0.0217, against a LambdaCDM halo-scatter prediction of 0.0797 -- 0.1 sigma, i.e. indistinguishable)

