====================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- item 117
====================================================================================================================
  The bound the item asked for, in the two channels the residual actually has (best-distance galaxies, both footings):

     WITHIN a galaxy   intrinsic = 0.036 +- 0.008 dex  -- an upper bound, at the size of the disc-geometry correction
                                                          this estimator omits.  The tightest number in the hunt.
     BETWEEN galaxies  intrinsic = 0.078 +- 0.022 dex  -- the framework's zero-scatter prediction FAILS here.

  The standard answer -- 'the RAR's scatter is entirely observational, intrinsic consistent with zero' -- is
  reproduced here and then withdrawn, because the budget that produces it leans on SPARC's Hubble-flow distance
  allowance, a flat 30% envelope implying ~390 km/s peculiar velocities.  A distance error is a pure vertical shift
  in the RAR plane, so it is directly testable, and the test fails: galaxies with 5% distances and galaxies with
  30% distances have the SAME per-galaxy offset scatter (p = 0.006 against the quoted errors).  Restricted to the
  36 galaxies with TRGB, Cepheid or SN distances, an unexplained 0.078 dex per-galaxy offset survives.

  What that costs: either the galaxy-to-galaxy stellar M/L scatter is 0.19 dex, 1.7x the stellar-population value,
  or a_0 varies by 0.20 dex from galaxy to galaxy.  The framework forbids the second and therefore owes the first.
  LambdaCDM gets 0.080 dex for free from halo mass and concentration scatter, in the same channel, with no extra
  freedom -- indistinguishable from the residual.  So this is a bound, not a discrimination.  And the within-galaxy
  channel excludes BOTH predictions, which says it is measuring the modelling error they share, not either theory.

  By-product worth keeping: the RAR's own scatter MEASURES SPARC's Hubble-flow distance errors and finds them far
  smaller than the catalogue's conservative allowance.  Any error budget in this repository that propagates e_D/D
  as a Gaussian for Hubble-flow galaxies is overestimating that term.

